from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q061-120')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e):w.add(n,'Statistics',topic,'Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e)
add(111,'Statistical Inference',r'''Xᵢ are iid with density (1/(2θ))exp(−|x−θ|/θ), θ>0. Let Uₙ=ΣXᵢ/n, Vₙ=ΣXᵢ²/n and Sₙ²=Σ(Xᵢ−Uₙ)²/(n−1), n≥2. Which hold?''',[r'Uₙ is unbiased for θ.',r'Sₙ² is unbiased for θ².',r'Vₙ/3 is consistent for θ².',r'(Uₙ,Vₙ) is complete.'],'AC',['This is Laplace with location θ and scale θ.','To disprove completeness, combine Vₙ and Uₙ² so that their expectations cancel.'],r'''For Laplace(location θ,scale θ), EX=θ and Var X=2θ², so EX²=3θ². Hence EUₙ=θ, proving A, while ESₙ²=2θ², refuting B. By the law of large numbers Vₙ→3θ², proving C. Also EUₙ²=θ²+2θ²/n=(n+2)θ²/n. Therefore g(Uₙ,Vₙ)=Vₙ−[3n/(n+2)]Uₙ² has expectation zero for every θ, but is not almost surely zero: the continuous full-support sample can have observations near a nonzero common value, where the expression is nonzero for n≥2. This integrable nonzero function disproves completeness, so D fails.''',r'''A Laplace distribution with location μ and scale b has density exp(−|x−μ|/b)/(2b), mean μ, variance 2b², and finite moments of every order. Its scale parameter is not its standard deviation. The usual sample variance with denominator n−1 is unbiased for the population variance. The law of large numbers makes a sample second-moment average consistent for EX², so a suitable known factor can convert it into a parameter-square estimator. Completeness of a statistic means every integrable function of it with expectation zero for all parameter values must vanish almost surely. One nontrivial zero-mean function disproves completeness. Comparing expectations of a sample second moment and squared sample mean often supplies such a function, because the latter includes the mean square plus the mean's sampling variance.''',[('For Laplace(μ,b), find EX².','EX²=Var X+(EX)²=2b²+μ².'),('For iid mean μ,variance σ², compute E(X̄²).','Var X̄=σ²/n and EX̄=μ, so E(X̄²)=μ²+σ²/n.')])
add(112,'Statistical Inference',r'''One observation X has density θe^{−θ(x−1)} for x>1,θ>0. Test θ=1 against θ≠1. Which hold?''',[r'The likelihood is maximized at θ=1/(x−1).',r'The likelihood maximum is e^{−1}/(x−1).',r'The likelihood-ratio test rejects if (x−1)e^{−x}<k for some k>0.',r'The likelihood-ratio test rejects if x>c for some c>1.'],'ABC',['Write t=x−1>0 and maximize θe^{−θt}.','The likelihood ratio t e^{1−t} is small at both t near zero and large t.'],r'''Log likelihood is logθ−θt, with derivative 1/θ−t and negative second derivative −1/θ². The maximum is at θ=1/t and equals e^{−1}/t, proving A,B. The likelihood ratio is e^{−t}/(e^{−1}/t)=te^{1−t}=e²(x−1)e^{−x}. Thus rejection for a sufficiently small ratio is equivalent to C with a rescaled positive threshold. The ratio increases to its maximum at t=1 and decreases thereafter, tending to zero at both endpoints 0 and infinity. A nontrivial two-sided likelihood-ratio test therefore has two rejection tails, not just x>c, refuting D.''',r'''A likelihood-ratio test compares the maximum likelihood under the null with the unrestricted maximum, rejecting when their ratio is small. For one exponential-rate observation shifted by a known amount, maximization is done in the rate parameter, and the log likelihood is concave. A two-sided alternative often leads to a statistic that is small for both unusually short and unusually long observed waiting times. Multiplying a test statistic by a positive constant does not change the rejection region after adjusting the cutoff. The ratio's shape, not merely its expression, determines whether rejection is one-sided or two-sided. Check the parameter space and the data support before differentiating; here x>1 ensures the finite positive maximum θ=1/(x−1).''',[('For one Exp(rate θ) observation t=2, find its MLE.','The log likelihood logθ−2θ has score 1/θ−2, so the MLE is 1/2 and maximum likelihood e^{−1}/2.'),('For the ratio r(t)=te^{1−t}, locate its maximum.','r′(t)=e^{1−t}(1−t), so it increases for t<1, decreases for t>1, and has maximum 1 at t=1.')])
add(113,'Statistical Inference',r'''Independent samples X₁,…,X₇ and Y₁,…,Y₉ have continuous shift distributions F(x−μ),F(x−θ), with unique medians μ,θ. Let Rᵢ be Yᵢ’s rank in the combined sample and T=Σᵢ₌₁⁹Rᵢ. For testing μ=θ against μ>θ, which hold?''',['The maximum T is 115.','A right-tailed T test is appropriate.',r'Under H₀,E T=76.',r'Under H₀,P(R₁=1,R₉=16)=1/240.'],'D',['There are 16 ranks, and the largest nine are 8 through 16.','Under the null labelled ranks are exchangeable; under the alternative the Y sample tends smaller.'],r'''The maximal sum is 8+9+⋯+16=9(8+16)/2=108, not 115. Under μ>θ, the X observations are shifted right relative to Y, so Y ranks tend lower; rejection should be left-tailed, not right-tailed. Under H₀ each labelled rank is uniform on 1,…,16, with mean 17/2, so E T=9·17/2=153/2=76.5, not 76. Finally P(R₁=1)=1/16, and conditional on that R₉ is equally likely to occupy any of the other 15 ranks, giving joint probability 1/(16·15)=1/240. Only D holds.''',r'''The Wilcoxon rank-sum statistic sums the combined ranks belonging to one of two independent samples from continuous distributions, so ties occur with probability zero. Under the null of identical distributions, every allocation of ranks to sample labels is equally likely; each labelled observation has expected rank (N+1)/2, and two labelled ranks are uniformly distributed over distinct ordered pairs. The maximum sum of m selected ranks uses the top m ranks. Under a location-shift alternative, the direction of rejection depends on which sample's ranks are summed: if the other sample shifts upward, the summed sample's ranks become smaller. Rank tests are distribution-free under the null but their tail direction still requires interpreting the parameter inequality. Exact half-integer expectations should not be rounded in mathematical claims.''',[('For sample sizes 3 and 4, sum the four second-sample ranks. Find its null mean.','There are N=7 ranks with mean 4 each. The rank sum has expectation 4·4=16.'),('For two labelled observations among 10 exchangeable ranks, find P(first rank2,second rank7).','The first event has probability 1/10 and the second conditional probability 1/9, so the joint probability is 1/90.')])
add(114,'Statistical Inference',r'''X₁,…,Xₙ are iid N(θ,1). Which Bayes-estimator claims hold?''',[r'θ̂=ΣXᵢ/(n+τ²) for prior N(0,1/τ²), squared-error loss.',r'θ̂=ΣXᵢ/(n+τ²) for prior N(0,τ²), absolute-error loss.',r'θ̂=ΣXᵢ/(n+1/τ²) for prior N(0,1/τ²), absolute-error loss.',r'θ̂=ΣXᵢ/n for Jeffreys prior, squared-error loss.'],'AD',['Posterior precision is likelihood precision n plus prior precision.','Squared loss uses the posterior mean; absolute loss uses the median, which equals the mean for a normal posterior.'],r'''With prior variance v, posterior precision is n+1/v and mean m=ΣXᵢ/(n+1/v). The posterior is normal, so mean and median coincide. For v=1/τ², m=ΣXᵢ/(n+τ²), proving A and refuting C. For v=τ², m=ΣXᵢ/(n+1/τ²), so B is false. For the normal location model with known variance, Fisher information is constant in θ, making Jeffreys prior flat. The posterior mean is X̄, proving D. Claims B,C fail as general formulas, although special τ values could make their denominators coincide.''',r'''A Bayes estimator minimizes posterior expected loss. Under squared-error loss the minimizer is the posterior mean; under absolute-error loss it is a posterior median. A normal posterior is symmetric, so these two estimators coincide. Normal likelihood with known variance and normal prior is conjugate: posterior precisions add, and the posterior mean is a precision-weighted average of the data mean and prior mean. A prior variance v contributes precision 1/v, so reciprocal-variance notation must be handled carefully. Jeffreys prior is proportional to the square root of Fisher information; for an unrestricted normal mean with known variance it is constant, giving the sample mean under squared loss. An estimator formula is a general claim in the hyperparameter, not an accidental equality for one value.''',[('For n=2, prior θ~N(0,4), and observations 2,4 with variance1, find posterior mean.','Data sum is 6 and total precision 2+1/4=9/4. Mean is 6/(9/4)=8/3; posterior variance is 4/9.'),('For a posterior N(3,2), give Bayes estimators under squared and absolute loss.','The posterior mean and median are both 3, so both losses give estimate 3.')])
add(115,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'''Data have x=(−2,−1,0,1,2), yᵢ=xᵢ². Fit least-squares lines with and without intercept. Which hold?''',['Both lines have the same slope.','Both models have the same intercept.','The model with intercept passes through an observed point.','The model without intercept passes through an observed point.'],'AD',['Symmetry makes Σx=Σx³=0.','The mean y is 2, while the no-intercept fitted line must pass through the origin.'],r'''Σx=0,Σx²=10,Σxy=Σx³=0, and ȳ=10/5=2. With intercept the slope is Σ(x−x̄)(y−ȳ)/Σ(x−x̄)²=0 and intercept 2, giving ŷ=2. Without intercept, the slope is Σxy/Σx²=0 and the implicit intercept is zero, giving ŷ=0. A holds and B fails. Observed y values are 4,1,0,1,4, none 2, so C fails. The no-intercept line passes through the observed point (0,0), proving D.''',r'''In simple least-squares regression with an intercept, the fitted slope is the centered cross-product divided by the centered x sum of squares, and the intercept is ȳ−β̂x̄. The fitted line always passes through the sample centroid, but need not pass through any individual observed point. Without an intercept, the fitted line is constrained to pass through the coordinate origin and its slope is the uncentered ratio Σxy/Σx². Symmetry of predictor values around zero often makes sums of odd powers vanish. If the response is an even function, such as x², that symmetry can make both fitted slopes zero while their intercepts differ. Distinguish the sample centroid from an observed data point, and use the correct centered or uncentered formula for the fitted model.''',[('Fit a line with intercept to x=−1,0,1 and y=1,0,1.','The slope is zero by symmetry and intercept is 2/3. The centroid lies on the line, but none of the three data points does.'),('Fit without intercept to x=1,2 and y=2,2.','Slope is (1·2+2·2)/(1²+2²)=6/5. The line ŷ=1.2x does not pass through either observation.')])
add(116,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'''A full regression has intercept and four predictors X₁,…,X₄. The reduced model has intercept,Z₁=X₁+X₃,Z₂=X₂−X₄. Let βᵀ=(β₀,…,β₄), and test H₀:Lᵀβ=0 with iid N(0,σ²) errors. Which hold?''',[r'L=[[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[−1,0],[0,1]] is a possible choice.',r'The test statistic has F_{2,n−4} distribution under H₀.',r'Under the restrictions the residual sum of squares has σ²χ²_{n−3} distribution.',r'The unrestricted residual sum of squares has noncentral σ²χ²_{n−4} distribution.'],'AC',['The reduced predictors require β₁=β₃ and β₂=−β₄.','Count full-model and reduced-model parameters including the intercept.'],r'''The reduced mean is β₀+γ₁(X₁+X₃)+γ₂(X₂−X₄), so the restrictions are β₁−β₃=0 and β₂+β₄=0, exactly those given by A. The full model has 5 parameters, and the reduced model 3. Under H₀ and a full-rank design, F=[(SSE_R−SSE_F)/2]/[SSE_F/(n−5)]~F_{2,n−5}, refuting B. The restricted residual is a central normal projection of rank n−3 when the restrictions are true, so SSE_R/σ²~χ²_{n−3}, proving C. The unrestricted residual is central χ²_{n−5}, not noncentral with n−4 degrees, refuting D. The usual regression distribution claims require n>5 and full-rank designs.''',r'''A general linear hypothesis imposes q independent linear restrictions on regression coefficients. Under iid normal errors and a full-rank design, the full-model residual sum of squares divided by σ² is central chi-square with n−p degrees of freedom, where p counts all parameters including the intercept. The increase in residual sum of squares from q valid restrictions is independent central chi-square with q degrees, leading to an F_{q,n−p} test. The restricted model has p−q parameters and residual degrees n−p+q when the null is true. Under a false restriction its residual sum can become noncentral, but the correctly specified full model's residual remains central. Construct the restriction matrix by expanding the proposed reduced predictors and comparing their coefficients.''',[('A regression with intercept and two slopes tests equality of the slopes. Give F degrees of freedom.','There are p=3 full parameters and q=1 restriction. Under the null the statistic has F_{1,n−3} distribution.'),('A five-parameter full model is reduced to two parameters under a valid null. Find residual degrees in both models.','Full residual degrees are n−5, reduced residual degrees n−2, and the extra residual sum has 3 degrees of freedom.')])
add(117,'Regression and Multivariate Analysis',r'''A positive-definite covariance matrix is [[1,ρ,ρ],[ρ,1,ρ],[ρ,ρ,1]]. Which hold?''',[r'ρ may be −0.47.',r'The first principal component can explain only 32% of total variation for some ρ.',r'The second principal component can explain more than 32% of total variation for any ρ.',r'The first principal component has variance 1+2ρ for any ρ.'],'A',['The eigenvalues are 1+2ρ and 1−ρ with multiplicity two.','Principal components are ordered by decreasing eigenvalue, which changes at ρ=0.'],r'''The covariance matrix is (1−ρ)I+ρJ. Its eigenvalues are 1+2ρ on the all-ones direction and 1−ρ on its two-dimensional orthogonal complement. Positive definiteness requires −1/2<ρ<1, so −.47 is allowed, proving A. Total variance is 3, and the largest of three eigenvalues is at least their mean 1, so the first component explains at least 1/3>32%, refuting B. The second eigenvalue is always 1−ρ, whose explained fraction exceeds .32 only when ρ<.04; for instance at ρ=.5 it explains 1/6, refuting the literal “for any ρ” in C. When ρ<0 the largest eigenvalue is 1−ρ rather than 1+2ρ, refuting D. Thus A alone is true under the printed universal wording.''',r'''Principal component analysis diagonalizes a covariance matrix and orders components by decreasing variance, namely its eigenvalues. The total variance is the trace, and each component's explained fraction is its eigenvalue divided by that trace. An equicorrelation matrix in dimension d has eigenvalue 1+(d−1)ρ in the all-ones direction and 1−ρ with multiplicity d−1 on the orthogonal complement. Positive definiteness requires both types positive. Their ordering depends on the sign of ρ; a named formula for one eigenvalue does not automatically give the first principal component for every parameter value. The largest eigenvalue is at least the average, giving a universal lower bound 1/d for the first explained fraction. Distinguish an existential “for some” from a universal “for any” when evaluating possible percentages.''',[('For d=3 and ρ=.5, find explained fractions.','Eigenvalues are 2,.5,.5, with total3. Fractions are 2/3,1/6,1/6.'),('For d=3 and ρ=−.4, find the first-component variance.','Eigenvalues are .2,1.4,1.4. The first component has variance1.4, not .2, and explains1.4/3 of the total.')])
add(118,'Sampling and Design of Experiments',r'''A block design has incidence matrix N=[[1,1,1,0],[1,1,0,1],[1,0,1,1],[0,1,1,1]]. Which hold?''',['The design is incomplete.','The design is connected.','The design is balanced.','The design is orthogonal.'],'ABC',['Each block contains three of the four treatments.','Every treatment pair occurs together in two blocks; compare incidence with proportional row and column totals for orthogonality.'],r'''There are v=4 treatments and b=4 blocks. Every column sum is k=3<4, so the design is incomplete. Every row sum is r=3, and every distinct row pair has inner product λ=2; hence it is a balanced incomplete block design. All treatment pairs co-occur, so the concurrence graph is connected. More explicitly NNᵀ=I+2J and the treatment information matrix is C=3I−(1/3)NNᵀ=(8/3)I−(2/3)J, of rank 3=v−1. Orthogonality of treatment and block effects would require nᵢⱼ=rᵢkⱼ/N_total=9/12=3/4 for every cell, whereas entries are 0 or1. Thus D is false and A,B,C hold.''',r'''The incidence matrix of a binary block design records which treatment appears in each block. Row totals are treatment replications, and column totals are block sizes. A design is incomplete when blocks omit some treatments. A balanced incomplete block design has constant replication r, constant block size k, and constant concurrence λ for every pair of distinct treatments. Connectedness means all treatment contrasts are estimable; it can be tested by a connected concurrence graph or a treatment information matrix of rank v−1. Orthogonality of treatment and block effects is a different property: incidence must be proportional to the row and column totals after centering, corresponding to treatment/block independence. Balance does not imply orthogonality. Check both matrix dimensions and whether the design is binary before interpreting entries.''',[('Four blocks each omit one of four treatments. Find v,b,r,k,λ.','v=b=4, each treatment appears in3 blocks so r=3, each block has k=3, and each pair is absent only from two blocks, giving λ=2.'),('A complete block design has each of three treatments in each of two blocks. Is it connected and orthogonal?','All treatment pairs co-occur, so it is connected. Every incidence entry is1, equal to r_i k_j/N=2·3/6=1, so treatment and block effects are orthogonal.')])
add(119,'Probability Distributions',r'''Two parallel components have independent lifetimes Xⱼ exponential with mean1/j, j=1,2. Let R,h be system reliability and hazard. Which hold?''',[r'R(t)=e^{−2t}+e^{−t}−e^{−3t}.','The expected system lifetime is1.',r'h(1)=(2e+e²−3)/(e+e²−1).',r'h(3)=(4e³+e⁶−5)/(e³+e⁶−1).'],'AC',['A parallel system survives while at least one component survives.','Its lifetime is the maximum; use h=−R′/R and E T=∫₀∞R.'],r'''Rates are1 and2. The system fails by time t only if both components fail, so R=1−(1−e^{−t})(1−e^{−2t})=e^{−t}+e^{−2t}−e^{−3t}, proving A. Its expected lifetime is1+1/2−1/3=7/6, refuting B. Hazard h=(e^{−t}+2e^{−2t}−3e^{−3t})/(e^{−t}+e^{−2t}−e^{−3t})=(e^{2t}+2eᵗ−3)/(e^{2t}+eᵗ−1). At t=1 this is C. At t=3 the correct numerator is e⁶+2e³−3, differing from D, which is false.''',r'''Reliability R(t)=P(T>t) is the survival probability of a lifetime. A series system fails when its first component fails, so its lifetime is a minimum; a parallel system fails only when all components fail, so its lifetime is a maximum. Independence makes the probability of all component failures the product of their failure probabilities. For a continuous lifetime, hazard is f(t)/R(t)=−R′(t)/R(t), and the expected nonnegative lifetime equals the integral of reliability. Exponential mean and rate are reciprocals. Even when components have constant hazards, a parallel system's hazard generally varies with time, because the surviving system may consist of different combinations of active components. Use inclusion–exclusion for its reliability and differentiate the resulting expression carefully.''',[('Two independent Exp(1) components run in parallel. Find mean system lifetime.','Reliability is2e^{−t}−e^{−2t}; integrating gives2−1/2=3/2.'),('The same components run in series. Find reliability and mean.','Both must survive, giving e^{−t}e^{−t}=e^{−2t}. The system is Exp(2) with mean1/2.')])
add(120,'Stochastic Processes',r'''An M/M/1 queue has arrival rate3 and exponential service times with mean1/9. Let Pₙ be the long-run probability of n customers in the system. Which hold?''',[r'P₀=1/3.',r'P₁=2/9.','The average number in the system is1.',r'The average time in the system is1/6.'],'BD',['The service rate is9, so utilization isρ=3/9.','Use Pₙ=(1−ρ)ρⁿ, L=ρ/(1−ρ), W=1/(μ−λ).'],r'''λ=3,μ=9 andρ=1/3<1, so a stationary distribution exists. P₀=1−ρ=2/3, refuting A. P₁=(2/3)(1/3)=2/9, proving B. Mean number L=ρ/(1−ρ)=(1/3)/(2/3)=1/2, refuting C. Mean system time W=1/(μ−λ)=1/6, proving D; Little's law checks L=λW=3/6=1/2. System time includes both waiting and service.''',r'''An M/M/1 queue has Poisson arrivals with rate λ, independent exponential service times with rate μ, and one server. The stable case requires utilization ρ=λ/μ<1. Its number-in-system stationary distribution is geometric, Pₙ=(1−ρ)ρⁿ, obtained from birth–death balance equations. The mean number is L=ρ/(1−ρ), and mean total time is W=1/(μ−λ). Little's law L=λW connects these steady-state quantities. Waiting time excludes the service duration and equals W_q=W−1/μ, while system time includes it. A stated service mean1/μ must be inverted to obtain the service rate. Confusing idle probability1−ρ with utilizationρ or average queue length with average system population leads to different answers.''',[('For arrival rate2 and service rate5, find idle probability and mean system time.','ρ=2/5, so P₀=3/5 and W=1/(5−2)=1/3.'),('For those rates, find mean number and waiting time excluding service.','L=λW=2/3. W_q=1/3−1/5=2/15; mean queue length is λW_q=4/15.')])
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
